<%#
  One row of the census table.

  Rendered both server-side and inside the `<template>` the add button clones,
  so there is exactly one definition of what a row looks like.

  Every input carries its own `aria-label` — a column header alone does not
  name a form control, and "Email" repeated twenty times tells a screen-reader
  user nothing about which person they are editing.

  For the same reason every field is built with `label: false`. Decidim's form
  builder otherwise wraps each input in a visible label repeating the column
  header, so a cell read "First name | First name [Ada]", and it renders the
  error itself — which, together with the one this partial used to add, printed
  "cannot be blank" twice per failing cell. The mobile stacked layout still
  names each cell: that is what `data-label` on the `<td>` is for, and the table
  is now `table-stacked`, which shows it below `md` and hides it above.

  Note that no input sets the HTML `required` attribute: there is always a
  spare blank row, and the browser would refuse to submit the form because of
  a row nobody meant to fill in. `aria-required` states the intent and the
  server enforces it.

  Locals: `f` (fields_for builder), `member`, `columns`, `required`,
  `disabled`, `row`.
%>
<tr class="js-census-member-row" data-census-member-row>
  <% columns.each do |field| %>
    <%
      attribute = Decidim::Elections::Vocdoni::CensusMember.attribute_for(field)
      label = Decidim::Elections::Vocdoni::CensusMember.field_label(field)
      invalid = member.errors[attribute].any?
      options = {
        disabled:,
        label: false,
        class: "input#{" is-invalid-input" if invalid}",
        aria: {
          label: t("decidim.elections.vocdoni.admin.census.members.cell_label", field: label, row:),
          required: required.include?(field).to_s,
          invalid: invalid.to_s
        },
        autocomplete: "off"
      }
    %>
    <td data-label="<%= label %>">
      <% if field == "birthDate" %>
        <%= f.date_field attribute, **options %>
      <% elsif field == "email" %>
        <%= f.email_field attribute, **options %>
      <% elsif field == "phone" %>
        <%# `text_field` with a tel type rather than `telephone_field`: Decidim's
            form builder does not wrap the latter, so it renders neither the
            error nor `label: false` — a phone cell was the one column that
            could fail silently. %>
        <%= f.text_field attribute, type: "tel", **options %>
      <% elsif field == "weight" %>
        <%= f.number_field attribute, min: 1, step: 1, **options %>
      <% else %>
        <%= f.text_field attribute, **options %>
      <% end %>
    </td>
  <% end %>

  <td class="table-list__actions" data-label="<%= t("decidim.elections.vocdoni.admin.census.members.remove") %>">
    <%#
      Hidden inputs live in a cell, not loose in the row: anything between
      `<tr>` and `<td>` is hoisted out of the table by the HTML parser.

      Values this election does not use travel back untouched. A national ID
      that came in with a CSV import must not be wiped just because nothing
      authenticates on it today — and the same goes for voting power while
      weighting is switched off.
    %>
    <%= f.hidden_field :id %>
    <% (Decidim::Elections::Vocdoni::CensusMember::FIELDS - columns).each do |field| %>
      <%= f.hidden_field Decidim::Elections::Vocdoni::CensusMember.attribute_for(field) %>
    <% end %>

    <%#
      A checkbox rather than a button: without JavaScript, ticking it and
      saving is how a row is removed, and with JavaScript the row is struck
      through while the box keeps the intent for the server.

      `label: false`, like every other field in this row. The box used to carry
      a visible "Remove" of its own so that the form builder would not name it
      after the attribute ("Deleted" — wrong word, wrong tense), but the cell
      is already named twice: by the column header above `md` and by the
      `data-label` on this `<td>` below it. On a phone that printed
      "Remove Remove", one from each. The `aria-label` carries the row number,
      so the accessible name is still unique per row and still says what the
      box does.
    %>
    <%= f.check_box :deleted,
                    disabled:,
                    label: false,
                    data: { "census-member-remove": true },
                    aria: { label: t("decidim.elections.vocdoni.admin.census.members.remove_label", row:) } %>

    <%#
      Shown by the browser the moment the box is ticked. The row is dimmed and
      struck through as well, but that is decoration: this sentence is what
      states, in words, that a row still sitting in front of the admin is on
      its way out.
    %>
    <%#
      No `block` utility class on this: `[hidden]` is a user-agent rule with no
      specificity at all, so any `display` declaration silently beats it. The
      display is set from the stylesheet instead, where the `[hidden]` case is
      stated alongside it.
    %>
    <span class="text-xs text-alert font-semibold mt-1"
          data-census-member-removed-note hidden>
      <%= t("decidim.elections.vocdoni.admin.census.members.will_be_removed") %>
    </span>
  </td>
</tr>
